<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Dashes, Brackets and Hyphens - When to use</h1>
  <div class="info-stack">
    <section class="info-card card-dash">
      <h2>The Dash (–)</h2>
      <p>Dashes can replace commas or brackets for extra detail or dramatic effect.</p>
      <p>Ex: My brother – the noisy one – broke the vase.</p>
      <p>Dashes can mark a pause, change of tone, or sudden thought.</p>
      <p>Ex: I opened the box – and gasped.</p>
      <p>To Show Afterthoughts or Conclusions.</p>
      <p>Ex:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>There was only one problem – we’d missed the bus.</li>
        <li>He knew what it meant – failure.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="info-card card-brackets">
      <h2>Brackets ( )</h2>
      <p>Brackets are used to add extra, non-essential information.</p>
      <p>If the bracketed information is removed, the sentence should still make sense.</p>
      <p class="rule-label">Examples:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>My sister (who lives abroad) is coming home.</li>
        <li>The cake (fresh from the oven) smelled delicious.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="info-card card-hyphen">
      <h2>The Hyphen (-)</h2>
      <p>Hyphens join words.</p>
      <p class="rule-label">Examples:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Re-cover (cover again) ≠ Recover (get better).</li>
        <li>Co-operate, re-enter, pre-school.</li>
        <li>Twenty-one, two-thirds, one-half.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
